<Project>

  <!--
    Repo-wide NuGet vulnerability audit.

    WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
    SSH.NET 2024.2.0 shipped GHSA-q939-rpr3-3284 (HIGH) in the SFTP delivery path and rode
    every single restore for months as `warning NU1903`. Nothing consumed that warning: CI's
    `dotnet restore ProcuLink.slnx` step is green with it, the Docker build is green with it,
    and no human reads restore output. A warning nobody sees is not a control. Escalating the
    HIGH/CRITICAL audit codes to errors is what turns it into one.

    NU1901 low · NU1902 moderate · NU1903 high · NU1904 critical.
    NuGetAuditLevel=high already suppresses NU1901/NU1902, so only 1903/1904 can ever fire.

    WHY MODE IS `direct` AND NOT `all` — this is load-bearing, do not "tighten" it casually.
    The repo has two HIGH advisories in TRANSITIVE packages that no first-party change can
    currently clear, both pre-dating this file:

      · Newtonsoft.Json 11.0.1              GHSA-5crp-9r3c-p9vr   (via Hangfire.Core 1.8.18)
      · System.Security.Cryptography.Xml 8.0.2  GHSA-37gx-xxp4-5rgx

    Under mode `all` those two fail the build on a clean checkout — including the Railway
    Docker image build, which would mean no deploys. Under mode `direct` the audit covers
    exactly the set this repo can actually act on: a direct <PackageReference> with a known
    HIGH advisory is upgradable by definition, which is the whole class the SSH.NET finding
    belonged to. Verify with `dotnet list ProcuLink.slnx package` plus the `vulnerable` and
    `include-transitive` switches before changing this to `all`; flip it only once those two
    are resolved.

    Setting `direct` explicitly is NOT redundant. The .NET 8 SDK (what CI and both Dockerfiles
    use) defaults to `direct` and ignores this property entirely, but the .NET 9+ SDK defaults
    to `all` — so on a developer machine running a newer SDK, omitting this line is what would
    fail the build on the two transitive advisories above.

    ESCAPE HATCH: if a HIGH advisory lands on a direct dependency with no fixed version yet and
    a deploy cannot wait, build with `-p:NuGetAudit=false` and open an issue the same day. Do
    not delete this file to unblock yourself.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <NuGetAudit>true</NuGetAudit>
    <NuGetAuditMode>direct</NuGetAuditMode>
    <NuGetAuditLevel>high</NuGetAuditLevel>
    <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);NU1903;NU1904</WarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
